From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 13:18:57 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: dwc: gs201-cp: add the zumapro (Pixel 9a) modem link

The Pixel 9a's S5300 modem hangs off zumapro's HSI1 GEN3A_0, the zuma
family's root complex, with the two-lane PHY that phy-zuma-pcie drives
as google,zumapro-pcie-gen3x2-phy. Add it as a second compatible of this
driver, selected through of_device_id data, with the PHY taken through
the generic PHY API as on gs201. What stays in the host, from Google's
GPL zuma driver (pcie-exynos-rc.c):

- RC mode, the soft power reset pulse and the slave NAK are written
  before phy_power_on(). zuma's CAL writes them inside its PHY
  configuration, which the PHY driver leaves to the host; pcie-gs201.c
  does the same on zuma's Wi-Fi link. ELBI is clocked then because the
  PHY's power_off returns its input clock (SOC 0x4000) to the
  oscillator, as Google's poweroff does.
- APP_XFER_PENDING is raised from training to the end of link_up, and
  around each read of the endpoint's configuration space, as
  exynos_pcie_rc_rd_other_conf() does.
- Two lanes are set in the port logic, and phy_calibrate() holds the
  PHY's PLL and bias on once the link has trained.
- The PHY leaves isolation at the first link_up and stays out, as
  stock's zumapro node has no use-phy-isol-en: link_down runs the
  controller's power-down steps and phy_power_off(), nothing else.
- There is no secure-ATU SMC: the host writes the unrolled outbound
  iATU, config space to bus 1 and the first 2 MiB of the memory window.
- I/O coherency only when samsung,sysreg is given.

The new fields go at the end of struct gs201_pcie_cp, and gs201's steps
are unchanged; the exported calls are the same, so cpif drives either
link.

Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig         |  12 +-
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c | 331 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 327 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
index 43efbf32e..f5226c8db 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
@@ -404,17 +404,19 @@ config PCI_EXYNOS
 	  functions to implement the driver.
 
 config PCIE_GS201_CP
-	tristate "Google gs201 PCIe for the modem (bring-up)"
+	tristate "Google gs201 and zumapro PCIe for the modem (bring-up)"
 	depends on ARCH_EXYNOS || COMPILE_TEST
 	depends on OF && PCI_MSI && HAVE_ARM_SMCCC
 	select PCIE_DW
 	select EXYNOS_PMU
 	select MFD_SYSCON
 	help
-	  Bring-up driver for PCIe GEN4A_0 of Google's gs201 (Tensor G2), the
-	  link to the Samsung S5300 modem. It powers the modem through its
-	  control lines and trains the link one debugfs step at a time, for
-	  testing on a bench; it does not register a PCI host yet.
+	  Bring-up driver for the link to the Samsung S5300 modem: PCIe
+	  GEN4A_0 of Google's gs201 (Tensor G2, Pixel 7) and GEN3A_0 of
+	  zumapro (Tensor G4, Pixel 9a), whose two-lane PHY is driven by
+	  PHY_ZUMA_PCIE. It powers the modem through its control lines and
+	  trains the link one debugfs step at a time, for testing on a
+	  bench, or on request of the modem driver (EXYNOS_MODEM_IF).
 
 config PCIE_GS201
 	tristate "Google gs201 PCIe controller (host mode)"
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
index 7990508fb..30eae683a 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
@@ -36,7 +36,8 @@
  *              it is disabled, the modem driver owns the lines)
  *   link_up    PHY out of isolation, PHY and controller setup, PERST#
  *              release, LTSSM, wait for L0 (Gen1 unless "link_up 3")
- *   atu        the secure-ATU SMC (config and memory windows)
+ *   atu        the secure-ATU SMC (config and memory windows); on zumapro
+ *              the host's own iATU
  *   id         read the endpoint's vendor/device ID through the config window
  *   bus        register the PCI host bridge and scan it (first time), with
  *              the DWC MSI controller aimed at the modem's MSI page; after
@@ -56,6 +57,28 @@
  * The "status" file only reads GPIO levels and the PMU isolation bit, plus
  * controller registers once the PHY is out of isolation (an ELBI access
  * while it is isolated hangs the SoC, as found on the Wi-Fi link).
+ *
+ * zumapro (Tensor G4, "google,zumapro-pcie-cp") connects the Pixel 9a's
+ * S5300 through HSI1 GEN3A_0, the zuma family's root complex, with the
+ * two-lane PHY of phy-zuma-pcie. Its steps follow Google's GPL driver for
+ * the zuma family (google-modules/soc/gs/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/
+ * pcie-exynos-rc.c: exynos_pcie_rc_poweron(), _establish_link(),
+ * _poweroff(), _rd_other_conf() and _prog_viewport_*()), and differ from
+ * gs201's in these places:
+ *  - RC mode, the soft power reset and the slave NAK are written before
+ *    the PHY is configured (zuma's CAL does them inside its PHY
+ *    configuration, which the PHY driver leaves to the host), and the PHY
+ *    is calibrated, its PLL and bias held on, once the link has trained;
+ *  - APP_XFER_PENDING is raised from training to the end of link_up and
+ *    around each read of the endpoint's configuration space;
+ *  - the port logic is set for two lanes;
+ *  - the PHY leaves isolation at the first link_up and stays out, as
+ *    stock's node has no use-phy-isol-en: link_down powers it down, and
+ *    its power_off returns its input clock to the oscillator first, so
+ *    that ELBI is clocked until the next power_on;
+ *  - no secure-ATU SMC: the host programs the unrolled outbound iATU;
+ *  - I/O coherency only when samsung,sysreg is given.
+ *
  * Copyright (C) 2021 Samsung Electronics Co., Ltd. (sequences)
  */
 
@@ -76,6 +99,7 @@
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
 #include <linux/pinctrl/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/soc/samsung/exynos-pmu.h>
@@ -126,6 +150,9 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(watch_lines, "Log every edge on the modem's cp2ap lines, with i
 #define CP_ELBI_L1_REQ_NAK_MASTER	BIT(4)
 #define CP_ELBI_MSTR_PEND_SEL_NAK	0x474
 #define CP_ELBI_DBI_L1_EXIT_DISABLE	0x1078
+#define CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING	0x074	/* zumapro */
+#define CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET_ALL		0xf	/* zumapro: written as a word */
+#define CP_ELBI_SLV_PEND_SEL_NAK	0x3d8	/* zumapro */
 
 /* PMU PHY control (PHY_CTRL_PCIE_GEN4_0): bit 0 set = isolation bypassed */
 #define CP_PMU_PHY_BYPASS		BIT(0)
@@ -142,6 +169,21 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(watch_lines, "Log every edge on the modem's cp2ap lines, with i
 /* Secure ATU: the outbound iATU is programmed by EL3 (Google's exynos_smc()) */
 #define CP_SMC_SECURE_ATU_SETUP		0x820020d8
 
+/* zumapro: the unrolled outbound iATU, DBI + 0x300000, 0x200 per region */
+#define CP_ATU_OB(r, reg)		(0x300000 + (r) * 0x200 + (reg))
+#define CP_ATU_CR1			0x00
+#define CP_ATU_CR2			0x04
+#define CP_ATU_LOWER_BASE		0x08
+#define CP_ATU_UPPER_BASE		0x0c
+#define CP_ATU_LIMIT			0x10
+#define CP_ATU_LOWER_TARGET		0x14
+#define CP_ATU_UPPER_TARGET		0x18
+#define CP_ATU_TYPE_MEM			0x0
+#define CP_ATU_TYPE_CFG0		0x4
+#define CP_ATU_ENABLE			BIT(31)
+#define CP_ATU_BUS(x)			(((x) & 0xff) << 24)
+#define CP_ATU_MEM_SIZE			SZ_2M	/* Google's limit */
+
 #define CP_PERST_DELAY_US		18000	/* modem: 18-20 ms, Wi-Fi 20 */
 #define CP_LINK_POLL_US			10
 #define CP_LINK_TIMEOUT_US		48000
@@ -191,6 +233,26 @@ static const char * const cp_input_names[CP_NUM_INPUTS] = {
 
 #define CP_ROOT_WIN_REGS	7	/* ARRAY_SIZE(cp_root_win_offs) */
 
+/**
+ * struct gs201_pcie_cp_data - what differs between the SoCs
+ * @zuma_ctrl: the zuma family's controller steps: RC mode, the soft power
+ *	reset and the slave NAK before the PHY configuration;
+ *	APP_XFER_PENDING from training to the end of link_up and around
+ *	endpoint configuration reads; two lanes in the port logic; and
+ *	phy_calibrate() once the link has trained
+ * @phy_bypass_once: the PHY leaves isolation at the first link_up and
+ *	stays out until the driver goes away
+ * @host_atu: no secure-ATU SMC; the host programs the outbound iATU
+ * @sysreg_optional: without samsung,sysreg the link runs without I/O
+ *	coherency
+ */
+struct gs201_pcie_cp_data {
+	bool zuma_ctrl;
+	bool phy_bypass_once;
+	bool host_atu;
+	bool sysreg_optional;
+};
+
 struct gs201_pcie_cp {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct dw_pcie pci;		/* only for the DBI helpers */
@@ -248,6 +310,9 @@ struct gs201_pcie_cp {
 	struct pci_saved_state *ep_state;	/* its config after the scan */
 	u32 root_win[CP_ROOT_WIN_REGS];	/* root port windows after the scan */
 	bool root_win_saved;
+	const struct gs201_pcie_cp_data *data;
+	phys_addr_t cfg_phys;		/* host_atu: the config window */
+	resource_size_t cfg_size;
 };
 
 /*
@@ -472,10 +537,21 @@ static int cp_cfg_read(struct pci_bus *bus, unsigned int devfn, int where,
 {
 	struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp = bus->sysdata;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	bool xfer;
 	int ret;
 
 	raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&cp->cfg_lock, flags);
+	/*
+	 * zumapro: as Google's exynos_pcie_rc_rd_other_conf(), hold the link
+	 * out of L1 while an endpoint read is in flight. cfg_ep_ok means the
+	 * link is up, so ELBI is reachable.
+	 */
+	xfer = cp->data->zuma_ctrl && !pci_is_root_bus(bus) && cp->cfg_ep_ok;
+	if (xfer)
+		cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING, 1);
 	ret = pci_generic_config_read(bus, devfn, where, size, val);
+	if (xfer)
+		cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING, 0);
 	raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cp->cfg_lock, flags);
 
 	/*
@@ -937,6 +1013,54 @@ static void cp_ctrl_irq(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, bool on)
 	cp->ctrl_irq_on = on;
 }
 
+/*
+ * zumapro: exynos_pcie_rc_poweroff() from PERST# on, while the PHY still
+ * runs: LTSSM off, APP_XFER_PENDING dropped, the controller's interrupts
+ * off and a soft power reset pulse; then phy_power_off(), which returns the
+ * PHY's input clock to the oscillator and powers the PHY down. ELBI stays
+ * clocked from there to the next phy_power_on(), whose controller steps
+ * come before the PHY's (cp_zuma_link_prepare()). Also the cleanup after a
+ * failed try.
+ */
+static void cp_zuma_phy_off(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
+{
+	u32 val;
+
+	if (!cp->phy_on)
+		return;
+	gpiod_direction_output(cp->perst, 1);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_LTSSM_EN, 0);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING, 0);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ0_EN, 0);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ1_EN, 0);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ2_EN, 0);
+	val = cp_elbi_read(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET, val & ~CP_ELBI_SOFT_PWR_RESET);
+	usleep_range(20, 40);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET, val | CP_ELBI_SOFT_PWR_RESET);
+	phy_power_off(cp->phy);
+	cp->phy_on = false;
+}
+
+/*
+ * zumapro: the link down with the PHY powered down but left out of
+ * isolation, as stock. DBI is closed to readers until the next link_up.
+ */
+static void cp_zuma_link_down(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
+{
+	bool was_on = cp->phy_on;
+
+	cp_ctrl_irq(cp, false);		/* reads ELBI: before anything else */
+	cp_cfg_allow(cp, false, false);
+	cp_msi_irqs(cp, false);
+	cp_zuma_phy_off(cp);
+	cp_dbi_allow(cp, false);
+	if (was_on)
+		dev_info(cp->dev, "link down, PHY powered down\n");
+	cp->link_up = false;
+	cp->atu_done = false;
+}
+
 /*
  * exynos_pcie_rc_poweroff(): PERST#, LTSSM off, a soft power reset of the
  * controller, PHY power-down; then isolation, as on suspend.
@@ -946,6 +1070,11 @@ static void cp_link_down(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
 	bool was_inited = cp->phy_inited;
 	u32 val;
 
+	if (cp->data->phy_bypass_once) {
+		cp_zuma_link_down(cp);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	cp_ctrl_irq(cp, false);		/* reads ELBI: before anything else */
 	cp_cfg_allow(cp, false, false);
 	cp_msi_irqs(cp, false);
@@ -1016,12 +1145,78 @@ static int cp_link_settle(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * zumapro: exynos_pcie_rc_poweron() and _establish_link() up to the LTSSM.
+ * The CAL writes RC mode, the soft power reset and the slave NAK in the
+ * middle of its PHY configuration, after the external PLL has locked; the
+ * PHY driver leaves ELBI to the host, so they come first, as pcie-gs201.c
+ * does on zuma's Wi-Fi link. ELBI is clocked here: the PHY is out of
+ * isolation, and it is powered down with its input clock on the
+ * oscillator (boot, or the last phy_power_off()).
+ */
+static int cp_zuma_link_prepare(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = &cp->pci;
+	u32 val;
+	int ret;
+
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_DEVICE_TYPE, CP_ELBI_DEVICE_TYPE_RC);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET_ALL);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET,
+		      CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET_ALL & ~CP_ELBI_SOFT_PWR_RESET);
+	fsleep(10);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET, CP_ELBI_SOFT_RESET_ALL);
+	fsleep(10);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_SLV_PEND_SEL_NAK, 1);
+
+	ret = phy_power_on(cp->phy);	/* power-down clear, CAL, lock checks */
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	cp->phy_on = true;
+
+	/* "EQ off" before PERST# release, as Google (again in cp_setup_dbi()) */
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, GEN3_RELATED_OFF, 0x12000);
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(cp->perst, 0);
+	fsleep(CP_PERST_DELAY_US);
+
+	cp_elbi_rmw(cp, CP_ELBI_REQ_EXIT_L1_MODE, 0,
+		    CP_ELBI_APP_REQ_EXIT_L1 | CP_ELBI_L1_REQ_NAK_MASTER);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_LINKDOWN_RST_SEL, CP_ELBI_LINKDOWN_RST_MANUAL);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING, 1);	/* to the end of link_up */
+	cp_elbi_rmw(cp, CP_ELBI_QCH_SEL, CP_ELBI_QCH_GATING_MASK, 0);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_MSTR_PEND_SEL_NAK, 1);
+	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_DBI_L1_EXIT_DISABLE, 1);
+
+	cp_setup_dbi(cp, speed);
+	if (cp->sysreg)
+		cp_set_iocc(cp, true);
+
+	if (cp->num_lanes == 2) {
+		val = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, PCIE_PORT_LINK_CONTROL);
+		val = (val & ~PORT_LINK_MODE_MASK) | PORT_LINK_MODE_2_LANES;
+		dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, PCIE_PORT_LINK_CONTROL, val);
+		val = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, PCIE_LINK_WIDTH_SPEED_CONTROL);
+		val = (val & ~PORT_LOGIC_LINK_WIDTH_MASK) |
+		      PORT_LOGIC_LINK_WIDTH_2_LANES;
+		dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, PCIE_LINK_WIDTH_SPEED_CONTROL, val);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 /* exynos_pcie_rc_establish_link(), one try */
 static int cp_link_try(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed, bool last)
 {
 	u32 val;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (cp->data->zuma_ctrl) {
+		ret = cp_zuma_link_prepare(cp, speed);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		goto ltssm;
+	}
+
 	ret = phy_power_on(cp->phy);	/* power-down clear, CAL, lock checks */
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -1055,6 +1250,7 @@ static int cp_link_try(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed, bool last)
 	cp_setup_dbi(cp, speed);
 	cp_set_iocc(cp, true);
 
+ltssm:
 	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_LTSSM_EN, 1);
 	ret = readl_poll_timeout(cp->elbi + CP_ELBI_LTSSM_STATE, val,
 				 FIELD_GET(CP_ELBI_LTSSM_MASK, val) ==
@@ -1080,6 +1276,10 @@ static int cp_link_up(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed)
 	if (cp->has_lines && !cp->cp_powered)
 		dev_warn(cp->dev, "modem not powered (cp_on): expect no link\n");
 
+	/* zumapro: the PHY stays initialized; only a powered-down one trains */
+	if (cp->data->phy_bypass_once && cp->phy_on)
+		return -EBUSY;
+
 	if (!cp->phy_inited) {
 		/* PERST# asserted and CLKREQ# in its function before the PHY */
 		gpiod_direction_output(cp->perst, 1);
@@ -1094,19 +1294,32 @@ static int cp_link_up(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed)
 			return ret;
 		cp_dbi_allow(cp, true);
 		cp->phy_inited = true;
+	} else if (cp->data->phy_bypass_once) {
+		cp_dbi_allow(cp, true);
 	}
 
 	for (try = 1; try <= CP_LINK_TRIES; try++) {
 		ret = cp_link_try(cp, speed, try == CP_LINK_TRIES);
 		if (!ret)
 			break;
+		if (cp->data->zuma_ctrl) {
+			cp_zuma_phy_off(cp);
+			continue;
+		}
 		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(cp->perst, 1);
 		cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_LTSSM_EN, 0);
 		phy_power_off(cp->phy);
 		cp->phy_on = false;
 	}
-	if (ret)
+	if (ret) {
+		if (cp->data->phy_bypass_once)	/* PHY down: DBI closed */
+			cp_dbi_allow(cp, false);
 		return ret;
+	}
+
+	/* zumapro: PLL and bias held on (Google's poweron, channel 0) */
+	if (cp->data->zuma_ctrl)
+		phy_calibrate(cp->phy);
 
 	cap = dw_pcie_find_capability(pci, PCI_CAP_ID_EXP);
 	lnksta = cap ? dw_pcie_readw_dbi(pci, cap + PCI_EXP_LNKSTA) : 0;
@@ -1118,6 +1331,8 @@ static int cp_link_up(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed)
 	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ0, cp_elbi_read(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ0));
 	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ1, cp_elbi_read(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ1));
 	cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ2, cp_elbi_read(cp, CP_ELBI_IRQ2));
+	if (cp->data->zuma_ctrl)
+		cp_elbi_write(cp, CP_ELBI_APP_XFER_PENDING, 0);
 	cp->link_up = true;
 	cp_cfg_allow(cp, true, false);
 	cp_ctrl_irq(cp, true);
@@ -1125,6 +1340,57 @@ static int cp_link_up(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp, unsigned int speed)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * zumapro: exynos_pcie_rc_prog_viewport_cfg0() and _mem_outbound(), in
+ * place of the secure-ATU SMC. Region 0 maps the config window to bus 1
+ * (the modem, device 0), region 1 the first 2 MiB of the memory window,
+ * Google's limit. The controller's soft power reset clears both, so this
+ * runs after every link_up.
+ */
+static int cp_host_atu(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = &cp->pci;
+	struct resource_entry *win;
+	u64 cpu, pci_addr;
+
+	win = resource_list_first_type(&cp->bridge->windows, IORESOURCE_MEM);
+	if (!win)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	cpu = win->res->start;
+	pci_addr = cpu - win->offset;
+
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_LOWER_BASE),
+			   lower_32_bits(cp->cfg_phys));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_UPPER_BASE),
+			   upper_32_bits(cp->cfg_phys));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_LIMIT),
+			   lower_32_bits(cp->cfg_phys + cp->cfg_size - 1));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_LOWER_TARGET), CP_ATU_BUS(1));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_UPPER_TARGET), 0);
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_CR1), CP_ATU_TYPE_CFG0);
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(0, CP_ATU_CR2), CP_ATU_ENABLE);
+
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(1, CP_ATU_CR1), CP_ATU_TYPE_MEM);
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(1, CP_ATU_LOWER_BASE),
+			   lower_32_bits(cpu));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(1, CP_ATU_UPPER_BASE),
+			   upper_32_bits(cpu));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(1, CP_ATU_LIMIT),
+			   lower_32_bits(cpu + CP_ATU_MEM_SIZE - 1));
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, CP_ATU_OB(1, CP_ATU_LOWER_TARGET),
+			   lower_32_bits(pci_addr));
